# Connecting the Pipelex workshop, and its key

Read this when an MCP-backed skill sends you here: because one of the {% if platform == "agent-skills" %}workshop's{% else %}plugin's{% endif %} `mthds_*` tools is absent from the session, or because a call returned a problem about the API key, `auth.missing_credential`, `auth.invalid_credential` or `auth.credential_expired`. It says what to tell the user on {{ harness_name }}, and nothing more: whether the skill stops there or carries on without the tool is the skill's own rule, which this file changes in neither direction.

## The tool is absent

Tell the user, in one line: *"{% if platform == "agent-skills" %}The Pipelex workshop isn't connected — on {{ harness_name }} it is an MCP server connected by whoever set this agent up, and no skill can start it: ask them to connect it, then retry.{% else %}The Pipelex plugin's MCP server isn't connected —{% if platform == "mistral-vibe" %} on {{ harness_name }} the plugin cannot start the local workshop (`{{ mcp_server.command }} {{ mcp_server.args | join(" ") }}`) itself, so it is one entry in your configuration. First, in `~/.vibe/config.toml`, or `$VIBE_HOME/config.toml` when `VIBE_HOME` is set, delete the `mcp_servers = []` line a new configuration holds, or rewrite a list on one `mcp_servers = [...]` line as `[[mcp_servers]]` tables, and delete any `pipelex` server already there, since either leftover stops {{ harness_name }} from starting. Then append the plugin's `mcp/vibe-mcp.toml` to the end of that file (`cat "${VIBE_HOME:-$HOME/.vibe}/plugins/pipelex/mcp/vibe-mcp.toml" >> "${VIBE_HOME:-$HOME/.vibe}/config.toml"`) and start a new {{ harness_name }} session.{% else %} the plugin manifest spawns the local workshop (`{{ mcp_server.command }} {{ mcp_server.args | join(" ") }}`), so its absence usually means `node`/`npx` is unavailable or the spawn failed. Check the plugin's MCP connection{% if platform == "claude" %} (`/mcp`){% endif %}.{% endif %}{% endif %}"*

The Pipelex connector's `pipelex_*` tools, when the session has them, belong to a different server: their presence does not mean the {% if platform == "agent-skills" %}workshop{% else %}plugin's server{% endif %} is connected, and they do not stand in for its `mthds_*` tools.

## Where the key comes from

{% if platform == "agent-skills" -%}
The server authenticates to the API with **`PIPELEX_API_KEY`**, which it is given by whoever connected it to {{ harness_name }}: nothing in this session, an exported variable included, reaches it. Ask the user to have a key given to the server through whoever connected the workshop, a new one from app.pipelex.com if they need one. Never read a credentials file or print a key yourself.
{%- else -%}
The server authenticates to the API with **`PIPELEX_API_KEY`**. It uses the key its host hands it, {% if platform == "mistral-vibe" %}which on {{ harness_name }} is only one written into the `env` table of its `[[mcp_servers]]` entry, since {{ harness_name }} passes a stdio MCP server only a few basic variables of your shell, such as `HOME` and `PATH`, and none of your `PIPELEX_` ones{% elif platform == "claude" %}which on {{ harness_name }} is the key entered in the **plugin configuration** when the plugin was enabled, kept in the OS keychain and exported for the server by its launcher, or else a `PIPELEX_API_KEY` exported in the shell {{ harness_name }} starts from, which Claude Desktop, launched from the desktop, never has{% else %}which on {{ harness_name }} is a `PIPELEX_API_KEY` exported in the shell {{ harness_name }} starts from, forwarded by name{% endif %}. When the host hands it none, it reads the key saved in `~/.pipelex/.env`, or in the `.env` of `PIPELEX_HOME` when that variable reaches it{% if platform == "mistral-vibe" %}, which on {{ harness_name }} also takes the entry's `env` table{% endif %}, afresh on every call, so a key saved while the session runs is used from the next call with no restart.

So a problem about the key is answered by the workshop's login, which the user runs themselves in a terminal: **`npx -y @pipelex/mcp@latest login`** opens the browser on app.pipelex.com, mints a key for the account's current organization and saves it in that file (`--paste` reads one created in the app instead, for a machine with no browser). The pipelex CLI's `pipelex login` saves to the same file, so a key it saved counts too. When the tool's summary says the host's own key was refused, the saved one is never consulted until that key is removed or replaced {% if platform == "mistral-vibe" %}in the entry's `env` table{% elif platform == "claude" %}in the plugin's configuration or the shell, whichever holds it{% else %}in the shell {{ harness_name }} starts from{% endif %}. Never read that file or print a key yourself.

The plugin's validation hook reads `PIPELEX_API_KEY` from {% if platform == "claude" %}the plugin configuration or the shell{% else %}{{ harness_name }}'s environment{% endif %} alone, never from that file, so with only a saved key it still lints and formats each edit but skips validating it against the API{% if platform == "mistral-vibe" %}: exporting `PIPELEX_API_KEY` in the shell {{ harness_name }} starts from restores that stage{% endif %}.
{%- endif %}

`auth.forbidden` is not about the key: the platform refused the action for the role the key's owner holds in the organization, and its `detail` names that role and what the action needs. No login or new key changes a role, so relay it as it came and never send the user to the login for it.
